  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 322 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nActive weather returns on Tuesday night and into Wednesday as a \ndeveloping low pressure system over Colorado will bring the \npotential of significant rainfall to the area. Little has changed to \nthe previous few forecasts with the better potential for rainfall \noccurring on Wednesday evening and into Thursday. By Thursday \nafternoon, precipitation will begin to diminish, completely coming \nto an end by Thursday evening. At this time, QPF amounts are still \nuncertain, but there is still the potential for some embedded \nthunderstorms Wednesday evening which may produce some heavier \nrainfall. At this time, not expecting any severe thunderstorms or \nflooding concerns, but will continue to monitor over the next few \ndays. \n\nBeyond Thursday night, strong upper level ridging will return to the \nwestern US allowing for surface high pressure to build south out of \nCanada bringing a return to dry conditions through the weekend. With \nthis high, cold air advection will push in a much colder airmass \ninto the northern and central Great Plains. With 850 mb temperatures \nin the -3 to 3 C range on Friday, surface highs will struggle to \nrise out of the 50s. Highs will gradually moderate through next \nweekend, rising back into the 70s by next Sunday. For low \ntemperatures, this CAA will drop overnight temperatures into the \nupper 20s to low 30s across the Panhandle and northwest Nebraska on \nFriday morning. Widespread low temperatures in the low to mid 30s \nare then expected for Saturday morning. Given this colder airmass, \nfrost and freeze potential is likely by the end of the week. At this \ntime, headlines are not in place, but if this colder temperature \ntrend remains consistent and confidence increases, they may be \nnecessary. Stay tuned to the latest forecasts for the most up to \ndate information regarding frost/freeze potential.",
